# Milestone: M3
# Covers: a strand sleeping on the sleep queue is woken (its wake_us passes)
#         while another strand is currently running; urbi_step delivers the
#         wakeup at the next scheduler turn after the running strand yields or
#         completes (row 12 §3.3 — sleep-queue drain runs at the end of each
#         dispatch turn, after urbi_vm_dispatch_loop_until_yield returns).
#
# blocked: a chunk-top `,` group cannot share a declaration with its
#          arms.  The fixture needs one accumulator visible to a sleeper
#          and to a busy loop, which means `;` before `,` -- and the
#          parser refuses to mix the two in one group.  The single-strand
#          sleep path is covered by temporal/sleep_in_strand.chk and, in
#          C, by tests/rt/test_sched.c::sleep_parks_until_its_deadline.
#
# Everything below this line predates the re-foundation: it names
# tasks and entry points that no longer exist, and its account of
# what stands in the way has been superseded by the `blocked:` line
# above, which was probed against this branch.  Kept for the
# original urbiscript it carries.
# matrix-row: scheduler-wake
#
# Reason: observing that a sleeping strand wakes while another strand is
# running requires two concurrent strands.  The chk runner uses the urbi_vm_run
# REPL path (no realm); `,` (OP_FORK_DETACH) and sleep-based suspension are
# not available there.  The single-strand sleep path is covered by
# tests/chk/scheduler/wait_sleep_basic.chk; this test additionally requires
# parallel execution to verify the "wake while running" timing contract.
# Substantive coverage via test_step_driver.c (step_wake_at_with_wakeup_pending)
# until the Phase 0 runtime-internals test cleanup; no C-level coverage today.
#
# When M5 + urbi_step chk driver land, implement this by:
#   # Two strands: first strand does work (loops 100 iters); second sleeps briefly.
#   # After loop completes the sleeping strand should be scheduled (woken).
#   var log = ""
#   { var i = 0 | while (i < 100) i = i + 1 | log = log + "A" } ,
#   { sleep(0) | log = log + "B" }
#   -- step to quiescent --
#   log
#   --- expected output ---
#   [00000001] "AB"
